course: dictionary
lesson: 7031
title: Participles: verb forms that work like adjectives
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A participle is a form of a verb. It can help make tenses, or it can work like an adjective and describe a noun. Look at the word endings to spot it. The present participle usually ends in «-ing», as in running, eating and singing.
explain: «I have eaten my lunch.» The word eaten comes after have, so it is a past participle. The past participle often ends in «-ed», «-en» or has a special form, like walked, eaten, sung and gone. Have plus a past participle makes the present perfect tense.
explain: The passive uses a form of "be" plus a past participle, as in «The window was broken.» Here broken is the past participle, and it shows that something was done to the window. Participles also describe nouns: in «a broken toy», broken works like an adjective.
fact: A participle is a form of a verb.
fact: The present participle ends in «-ing»: running, eating, singing.
fact: The past participle often ends in «-ed», «-en» or has a special form: walked, eaten, sung, gone.
fact: «Have» plus a past participle is called the present perfect, as in «I have seen it».
fact: «Participle phrase» means a group of words built around a participle, and «present participle» and «past participle» are the names to learn for SATs grammar tests.
exercise: Which word is the past participle in «I have eaten my lunch.»? | *eaten | have | lunch | my
exercise: Which word is the present participle in «The dog is running in the park.»? | is | park | *running | dog
exercise: In «a broken toy», the word broken works like | a verb in the present tense | *an adjective | a noun | an adverb
test: Which word is the past participle of sing in «I have ___ a song»? | sang | *sung | singing | sings
test: In «The window was broken», broken is a | adverb | *past participle in a passive | noun | present participle
exam: Which sentence uses a present participle? | She has gone home. | The cake was eaten. | *He is laughing at the joke. | They walked to school.
